An adverb is a word that modifies or describes a verb, an adjective, another adverb, or an entire sentence.

examples:

quickly – The dog ran quickly. (modifies the verb ran)
very – She is very smart. (modifies the adjective smart)
yesterday – I called him yesterday. (modifies the verb called, indicates time)
here – Put the book here. (modifies the verb put, indicates place)
always – He always arrives early. (modifies the verb arrives, indicates frequency)
too – The coffee is too hot. (modifies the adjective hot, indicates degree)
never – She never lies. (modifies the verb lies, indicates frequency)
outside – The children played outside. (modifies the verb played, indicates place)
extremely – He was extremely tired. (modifies the adjective tired, indicates degree)
fortunately – Fortunately, no one was hurt. (modifies the entire sentence, indicates attitude)
